[2026-11-20T14:47:39.543Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_191","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/sds-224-cache/scrubadub-report.md <<'EOF'\nScrubadub 2.0.1 pins TextBlob to 0.15.3. On CPython 3.14.10 a fresh import emits \"'`SyntaxWarning: invalid escape sequence '\"'\\\\.'\"'` at `textblob/_text.py:218` and `:219`. With warnings promoted to errors the benchmark cannot run.\n\nReproduce in a fresh Python 3.14 virtual environment:\n\n```sh\npython -m pip install scrubadub==2.0.1\npython -W error::SyntaxWarning -X pycache_prefix=/tmp/scrubadub-import-repro -c '\"'import scrubadub'\n\"'```\n\nWith the declared dependency graph, the scanner currently works for us with normal warning handling. We kept the exact TextBlob pin downstream. Installing TextBlob 0.20.1 into an isolated overlay and running the same offline email detection through scrubadub with `-W error::SyntaxWarning` gave identical spans without the warnings. TextBlob previously addressed the warning in sloria/TextBlob#302.\n\nWould you test scrubadub'\"'s built-in detectors with current TextBlob, then replace \"'`textblob==0.15.3` with a supported range such as `textblob>=0.20.1,<0.21` or make TextBlob an extra for the detectors that use it? A fresh Python 3.14 import with `-W error::SyntaxWarning` would catch the regression.\n\nDownstream context: https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/pull/224\nEOF\ncat > /tmp/sds-224-cache/browser-commander-report.md <<'\"'EOF'\nBrowser Commander 0.35.0 resolves \"'`command-stream@2.6.0` and `links-notation@0.25.2` through its `''^2.0.2` and `''^0.25.2` ranges. Applications using the current releases directly get both generations of these packages. This keeps the 2.x terminal/capture dependency tree in installs that use the command-stream 3.x shell API.\n\nReproduce with Node 26.13.1 and npm 12.2.3 in a clean directory:\n\n```sh\nnpm init -y\nnpm install --ignore-scripts browser-commander@0.35.0 command-stream@3.0.0 links-notation@0.31.8\nnpm ls browser-commander command-stream links-notation\n```\n\nWe keep the transitive versions selected by Browser Commander. Replacing them with overrides would bypass its supported APIs. Would the project migrate to command-stream 3 and Links Notation 0.31? Command-stream 3 makes terminal/GIF dependencies explicit for capture users. Please exercise terminal recording and existing config/CLI Links Notation parsing during the migration.\n\n[command-stream 3.0.0 release](https://github.com/link-foundation/command-stream/releases/tag/js-v3.0.0) · [Downstream compatibility inventory](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/pull/224)\nEOF\ncat > /tmp/sds-224-cache/lino-i18n-report.md <<'\"'EOF'\nThe published \"'`lino-i18n@0.6.0` release specifies `lino-objects-codec: ''^0.9.6`. An application that directly uses codec 1.0.0 also installs a second decoder. Both copies use Links Notation 0.31.8.\n\nReproduce with Node 26.13.1 and npm 12.2.3 in a clean directory:\n\n```sh\nnpm init -y\nnpm install --ignore-scripts lino-i18n@0.6.0 lino-objects-codec@1.0.0\nnpm ls lino-objects-codec links-notation\n```\n\nWe keep the declared 0.9 compatibility range and test both consumers independently. Would a release test codec 1.0.0'\"'s encode/decode behavior with the existing translation extraction and roundtrip fixtures? If both majors work and older Node versions remain supported by this package, accepting both ranges would let applications on Node 26.13.1 share the current codec. Raising the runtime floor and moving to 1.x is also viable if aligned with the project's support policy.\n\n[Codec 1.0.0 release and runtime floor](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-objects-codec/releases/tag/js_v1.0.0) · [Downstream compatibility inventory](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/pull/224)\nEOF\ncat > /tmp/sds-224-cache/toolkit-report.md <<'EOF'\n\"'`@actions/artifact@6.3.1` uses `archiver@''^7.0.1`. This keeps `archiver-utils@5.0.2` and its deprecated `glob@''^10.0.0` in downstream installs. Archiver 8 removes the archiver-utils dependency from its core package and switches to named ESM classes. The artifact SDK'\"'s current ZIP builder calls \"'`archiver.create('\"'zip', options)\"'`, so a dependency override to 8 would break that code.\n\nTo inspect the graph with Node 26.13.1 and npm 12.2.3:\n\n```sh\nnpm init -y\nnpm install --ignore-scripts @actions/artifact@6.3.1\nnpm ls @actions/artifact archiver archiver-utils glob\n```\n\nOur composite action presently overrides only archiver-utils'\"' glob dependency to glob 14.0.6. SDK archive creation and extraction tests cover binary, empty, and nested files and the glob exclusion behavior. We'd like to remove the override.\n\nWould you use the Archiver 8 ESM ZIP API in the SDK's upload helper and verify the resulting archive with its existing roundtrip tests? Please also check \"'`npm ls archiver-utils glob` in the candidate graph for indirect archive-plugin dependencies.\n\n[Archiver 8 release](https://github.com/archiverjs/node-archiver/releases/tag/8.0.0) · [Archiver upstream glob report](https://github.com/archiverjs/node-archiver/issues/822) · [Downstream compatibility inventory](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/pull/224)\nEOF\nmkdir -p /tmp/sds-224-cache/archiver-check\nexport PATH=\"/tmp/sds-224-cache/bin:$PATH\"\nnpm --prefix /tmp/sds-224-cache/archiver-check install --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund archiver@8.0.0 > /tmp/sds-224-cache/archiver-check.log 2>&1 &&\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import('\"'/tmp/sds-224-cache/archiver-check/node_modules/archiver/index.js').then(module => console.log(Object.keys(module)))\\\" &&\nnpm --prefix /tmp/sds-224-cache/archiver-check ls archiver-utils glob --all\"","aggregated_output":"[ 'Archiver', 'JsonArchive', 'TarArchive', 'ZipArchive' ]\narchiver-check@ /tmp/sds-224-cache/archiver-check\n└── (empty)\n\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"