[2026-11-20T14:55:12.209Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_272","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('dev/log/issues/223/pulls/224/README.md')\ns=p.read_text().replace('Keep the previously captured Python requirements file as immutable evidence and review its pins in the dependency inventory.','Refresh the Python 3.14 requirements file specifically named in the issue. Its original version remains available at the baseline commit.').replace('Add a patch changeset','Add a major changeset for the Node engine floor')\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ncat >> dev/log/issues/223/pulls/224/README.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Version inventories\n\nRegistry metadata was collected on 2026-11-20 UTC. Baseline rows use the original merge base. Blank selections identify lock locations or packages removed by the new resolution. A newer registry version may be outside a consumer's supported range. The tables identify that consumer and, where npm records it, its declared range. The npm registry's \"'`latest` dist-tag for the NLP.js family points to an alpha even though a stable 4.x line is available.\n\n- [Root npm lock](npm-root.csv), [example app npm lock](npm-app.csv) and [artifact transport npm lock](npm-artifact.csv) include added, retained, hoisted and removed entries. The local temp-rimraf shim has no external dependencies. It remains in the example lock.\n- [Deno lock](deno.csv) includes peer-dependent npm locations and the consumers resolving each held version.\n- [Python](python.csv) covers the active requirements, hash lock, the Python 3.14 requirements file named by the issue, and isolated CI tooling. Dependabot now names both Python directories. Its pip fetcher looks at requirements content in `.txt` files, including this filename. [Dependabot pip file discovery](https://github.com/dependabot/dependabot-core/blob/main/python/lib/dependabot/python/shared_file_fetcher.rb) · [Original Python evidence at the baseline commit](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/blob/5c2804cf476e3eb4aed7ac09e2245e2617b447ab/dev/log/issues/102/pulls/103/research/requirements-python314.txt)\n- [GitHub Actions](github-actions.csv) lists each external action, hosted action digest and in-repository composite occurrence. The CodeQL bundle and the GitHub Action have independent release tags.\n- [Toolchains, images and binaries](toolchains.csv) covers external releases outside the dependency locks. Shell scripts and HTML use tools and asset packages from these existing ecosystems. The repository has no active Dockerfile, Cargo, Go, Gradle, Xcode or other native package manifest. Capacitor generates the Android and iOS projects from the app'\"'s npm pins.\n\n## Migration decisions\n\n- \"'`command-stream` 3 removes terminal and recording dependencies from its default installation. The sanitizer uses the shell tag and `command-stream/execa`. It does not call the moved PTY or recording APIs. Browser Commander still declares the 2.x range, so development installs retain a separate 2.x graph. [Command Stream 3 release](https://github.com/link-foundation/command-stream/releases/tag/js-v3.0.0) · [Browser Commander report](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/issues/193)\n- `lino-objects-codec` 1.0.0 keeps the existing codec integration and requires Node 26.13.1 and npm 12.2.2 or later. The project now uses Node 26.13.1 and npm 12.2.3 and has a major changeset for the Node support change. Node, Bun and Deno support the native `RegExp.escape` used by the NER vocabulary builder and changeset package matcher in place of two local implementations. Lino i18n still declares a separate 0.9 codec range. [Codec 1 release](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-objects-codec/releases/tag/js_v1.0.0) · [Lino i18n report](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-i18n/issues/45)\n- CPython stays on its latest 3.14 patch because current Presidio publishes `Requires-Python: >=3.11,<3.15`, plus caps on NumPy and preshed. Prior CI reached a native failure in `spacy.load(\"en_core_web_sm\")` from the constrained 3.15 graph. Use a hash-locked install and load the actual model when retesting. [Presidio metadata](https://pypi.org/pypi/presidio-analyzer/2.2.366/json) · [Upstream reproduction](https://github.com/data-privacy-stack/presidio/issues/2431#issuecomment-6673100519)\n- Java uses current Temurin 21 with a matching Gradle runtime. The generated project uses Gradle 8.14.3, Android Gradle Plugin 8.13.0 and Android SDK API 36. A Java 27 migration would require a Gradle 9 migration outside the current Capacitor template. Setup Java 6.3 makes annotations opt-in, so the workflow explicitly keeps the problem matcher. [Latest Temurin 21](https://github.com/adoptium/temurin21-binaries/releases/latest) · [Gradle Java matrix](https://docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/compatibility.html#java_runtime) · [Capacitor 8 migration](https://capacitorjs.com/docs/updating/8-0) · [Setup Java 6.3 release](https://github.com/actions/setup-java/releases/tag/v6.3.0)\n- Upstream consumers own the transitive terminal-formatting major transitions. The reviewed pure-ESM and output changes cover ANSI detection/styling, emoji patterns, string width, and sisteransi. There are no direct imports of these transitive libraries from shipped or CI code. Both npm example packaging and the runtime suite exercise the parent tools. [ansi-regex](https://github.com/chalk/ansi-regex/releases) · [ansi-styles](https://github.com/chalk/ansi-styles/releases) · [emoji-regex](https://github.com/mathiasbynens/emoji-regex/releases) · [string-width](https://github.com/sindresorhus/string-width/releases) · [strip-ansi](https://github.com/chalk/strip-ansi/releases) · [sisteransi](https://www.npmjs.com/package/sisteransi)\n- Both Playwright images use the registry digest for the locked Playwright 1.65.1 release. The workflow regression test checks the tag and digest in both jobs against the npm version. BuildKit’s canonical and fallback registry tags resolve to the same manifest digest. Betterleaks 1.10.1 ships the same configuration checksum and 203-file-independent rule count as the prior patch. [Playwright release notes](https://playwright.dev/docs/release-notes) · [Buildx release](https://github.com/docker/buildx/releases/tag/v0.39.1) · [Betterleaks release](https://github.com/betterleaks/betterleaks/releases/tag/v1.10.1)\n\n## Upstream reports and retained workarounds\n\n| Report | Reproduction and downstream decision |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Browser Commander #193](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/issues/193) | `npm ls` shows Command Stream 2.6.0 and Links Notation 0.25.2 under 0.35.0, alongside this project'\"'s newer direct dependencies. Keep upstream ranges pending the capture and config migration. |\n| [Lino i18n #45](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-i18n/issues/45) | \"'`npm ls` shows the 0.9 and 1.0 codecs installed side by side. Keep the i18n consumer'\"'s 0.9 range pending upstream roundtrip tests. |\n| [Actions Toolkit #2582](https://github.com/actions/toolkit/issues/2582) | The current SDK expects \"'`archiver.create`; Archiver 8 exports named archive classes and no longer resolves `archiver-utils` or its glob. Keep the targeted glob override until the SDK migrates. |\n| [Scrubadub #157](https://github.com/LeapBeyond/scrubadub/issues/157) | Scrubadub 2.0.1 pins TextBlob 0.15.3, whose two invalid escape sequences warn on CPython 3.14. The current TextBlob passed the same offline scanner smoke in an isolated overlay with `-W error::SyntaxWarning`. Keep the upstream-required pin in the real graph. |\n| [Existing upstream reports from PR 103](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/blob/main/dev/log/issues/102/pulls/103/upstream/reports.json) | Covers Node setup-cache ordering, Buildx failover, artifact download/ZIP behavior, the local temp cleanup adapter, Pages, Python wheels and workflow lint grammar. The scoped workarounds remain in place. |\n| [Lychee #2297](https://github.com/lycheeverse/lychee/issues/2297) | Current Lychee retry classification still misses connect/TLS resets. The existing bounded link recheck remains in the workflow. |\n\n## CI regression evidence\n\n| Finding | Evidence | Fix or disposition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Prettier failure after #222 | At baseline SHA `5c2804c`, main run 44753249623 reported formatting failures for `docs/COVERAGE.md` and `docs/case-studies/issue-128/research/open-source-pii.md` (log lines 786–789). `npm exec --prefix /tmp --package prettier@3.10.1 -- prettier --check <both files>` reproduced exit 1. [Failed main check](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/actions/runs/44753249623) | Commit `357434d` applies the exact locked formatter output. The same command and the full Prettier check pass. |\n| Lock audit errors reported in #212 | The two job logs both reported high-severity picomatch 4.0.7 advisory GHSA-fc5q-39xc-vjpf on line 216 with no fix. The advisory was withdrawn on 2026-11-18. [Advisory](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-fc5q-39xc-vjpf) · [Original security run](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/actions/runs/44210448243) | Baseline main security run 44753249634 and all three refreshed npm audits pass. The earlier audit error no longer reproduces against current registry data. [Main security](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/actions/runs/44753249634) |\n| Runtime matrix asserted a stale Bun version | Run 44756454326 reported an exact Bun 1.4.4 expectation at `tests/transient-links.test.js:127` after upgrading Bun to 1.4.5. [Job annotation](https://github.com/link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/actions/runs/44756454326/job/136357933397) | Commit `ac28d99` verifies every setup selects a fixed release in the supported major and the regression test passes on Node, Bun and Deno. |\nEOF\nsed -i '\"'s/203-file-independent rule count/203-rule catalog/' dev/log/issues/223/pulls/224/README.md\ngit status --short\nwc -l dev/log/issues/223/pulls/224/*\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
